Z||000009||DJRice||12-21-1999||08:28 AM||||That is really crappy....But isnt it funny the first things that go through your mind when you see something different about your car.  I had a car broken into and the first thing I thought when I opened the door was "I didnt leave this mess all over the floorboard."  The glove box was emptied and the change was removed from my ashtray.  <P>Look at the bright side, at least they didnt steal the whole car.   Good Luck getting her back in shape.||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||12-21-1999||01:00 PM||||Friends,<P>Although I might sound shady saying this, our little cars are the easiest that I have ever broken into.  Naturally, I have only broken in to mine and always in a pinch.  Now the background... an explanation of where I began to "break" into cars.  Years ago, I worked at a carwash.  On certain vehicles there is/was a function that would automatically lock the doors when the vehicle was put in gear.  On the first of these systems an automatic unlock system was not employed.  At a carwash where sometimes 400+ cars go through single-file on any given day we didn't have time to close the doors.  When the car is driven out, the door jams are dried and the doors closed in order to remove all excess water from the car.  That was when the problem started.  Now we had customers cars that were running with the doors locked and more cars coming thru behind them.  Not much time to get these cars out of the way, so we had an official tow-truck tool kit full of various slim-jims, wonderbars and the like.  So I have much experience with cars.  Any vehicle such as ours that does not have the windows framed by a door is rather easily accessed.  The only way that this can be combatted is to purchase a good alarm system.  Although they are rather expensive, I would much rather spend $300 to protect my several thousand $ investment.  <P>Although I am afraid to divulge much information, but I have broken into one of my Fiero's before with no tools at all.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000011||stsmithgt||12-21-1999||01:01 PM||||If you plan on "cleaning their clock", You must at least knock them out.  Probably break a bone or more to put them in a state of shock too.  It reduces the chance of them being able to press charges that will stick because your long gone and their in a daze when they wake up.  Head wounds work, but you don't want to kill the scum.  It's better for them to live in misery with permanent scars to remind them of their mistake and an example to their scumbag friends. By the way Merry Christmas.  I'm not sick- sick- sick -sick (at least that's what the voices in my head say) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||12-21-1999||01:27 PM||||William--Thanx for the info.  There are a couple of cars up the street I've had my eye on for a while.         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">Just kidding <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Richard Parnell||12-21-1999||01:58 PM||||DJRice-  As an aside be careful putting change in your console ashtrays.  When I first bought my Fiero I was having a hard time shifting (not unusual ) and opened up the console to take a look at the shift linkage.  Because the ashtrays have small slots in them some of the coins will fall down into the shift linkage area.  I pulled out over five dollers worth of quarters and dimes, some were wedged under moving parts.<P>And I also hate theives.  Someone slim Jimed my car a while ago and took a briefcase with wallet passport and worst of all irrepacable pictures of my son and very old pictures of my grandparents. The guy even had the nerve to call me and try to sell me the stuff back.  I was so upset instead of saying yes and then informing the police I started threating him and he hung up.||||reg||12
Z||000014||HF6582||12-21-1999||01:59 PM||||William,<BR>I agree the fiero is an easy car to break into. I've worked in the fire sevice for ten years, so I have had to get into various models of cars. The newer cars are using the double outside dew strip now and they are a B**** to get into.  On the fiero the glass has no frame so the window can be moved  enough to do your business.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000015||Racer 1||12-21-1999||02:51 PM||||Sorry 2 hear about that. I had my fiero almost busted into also about 4 years ago. The theives were dumb cause I had a Viper alarm with motion sensor and soft touch features ,ign kill,pager . Well the theives got pissed I guess and tried to throw a brick threw my rear window. The window held up only had some scratches the brick hit my hump on my rear decklid and flew back over my wing. I ran outside to see why my alarm was going off and then called the police. For about six months after that I slept on my couch with a Tec-9 9mm gun and a 30 round clip under the couch cushon. Specialy made infered sensor place in special locations around my house triggered a little bright flashing LED in my room with a little buzzer if someone was try to step up. But I park my car in the garage now so I can sleep easier at night  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. But I would advise you to get a good quality alarm like viper no cheap autozone ones.||||reg||13
Z||000016||ChadMan||12-21-1999||03:25 PM||||And I would advise you, Racer, to obtain a realistic-looking toy pistol to drop by the would-be thief's bullet-riddled body.  I see nothing wrong with shooting car thieves and/or vandals, but for some reason, judges and juries don't agree.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||12-21-1999||04:49 PM||||Reminds me of when I was going to school in Columbus this summer. My roomate always carried a 4-cell(4 d-size batteries) maglite. He kept it in the little space between his seat and the door. Then, whenever he got out of his car, in a "shady" area, he could just carry that flashlight with him. <BR>I remember once, we had taken a wrong road in whitehall, were goign down some alley, and while waiting at a stoplight, some freaky guy was coming towards us. Tony(my roomate)had that maglite out almost instantly. The guy just kept on going, I thought that light was a pretty cool idea. I have a HUGE 10lb. pipe wrench I carry around with me. Sits just behind and beside the drivers seat.<BR>I also have the pager on my Viper system. No false alarms from the system yet. But one night at 3am, while sleeping my pager started going off. I threw on a pair of shoes and grabbed a 1/2" thick 4 foot dowel rod. I crept outside, snuck up, and jumped around the corner to kick the crap out of whoever was messing my car. Turns out it was my roomates pulling a practicle joke, giggling as they hid under a neigboring van. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Patrick||12-21-1999||05:09 PM||||<BR><B>1/2" thick 4 foot dowel rod</B> ??<P>I hope this dowel rod is metal and not wood!<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000019||fiero56||12-21-1999||05:48 PM||||It was metal alright. One of the guys' fathers was a machinest, so we always had odd stuff laying around the apartment. I'm not sure what the exact material was since it seemed a little light, but we could never bend them.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Racer 1||12-21-1999||05:54 PM||||Chadman I wouldnt shoot the person I would just make him freeze in his tracks till police arrived. A person here in NM had a guy breaking into his shed and shot him in the leg he got off clean. People here are getting sick of dirtbags taking there stuff and half the time the cops take 15-30 min just to arrive unless its a major emergency. in some countries if you get cought stealing you loose a finger! ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Cliff Pennock||12-21-1999||06:00 PM||||I'm not sure if this is legal in the States (it sure as hell isn't legal here in Holland), but another good thief deterrent: most of the time they scan your car with a little flashlight to see if you have any valuables inside before they go through the "trouble" of actually breaking in. If you have a few 9mm bullets laying around, I'm pretty sure they'll skip your car...||||reg||1
Z||000022||stsmithgt||12-21-1999||06:37 PM||||Leaving bullets exposed in your car exposed isn't the problem.  It becomes illegal when the gun that uses those bullets is accessible. Basically you can't have both items available to each other in the vehicle.  When I had a pick-up.  I had a gun rack in it.  There usually was bullets and\or boxes of bullets on the dash or seat at all times unless I put guns in the rack.  At that time, all bullet must leave the area. If one of them(gun or bullets) is in a locked box, the rules may differ. I don't know. So I put them in the tool box in back. I tend to bend those rules a little bit myself.  I hate it when a lowrider comes up and asks me if I want to see their 9. (9mm) without having my 45 available as an option.||||reg||1
Z||000023||stsmithgt||12-21-1999||06:40 PM||||sorry about the bad grammer and typos <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||fiero56||12-21-1999||10:11 PM||||For us paintballers, we actually have to follow the same laws as regular guns. I put my gun in the trunk, and put my "bullets" on the passenger side floor. I'm not sure about where the co2 goes, I put it with the paint balls away from the gun.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Monkeyman||12-21-1999||10:33 PM||||When I cleaned up my car, I found over $7 in change and a house key under/near/around the shifter.  For those that intend to shoot an intruder, make sure he has a gun (like Chadman said) and empty the gun into him.  Kill him dead.  Dead men tell no tales.  <P>fiero56--They make 5- and 6-cell Maglites, too. I have a 5-cell and would have bought the 6-cell, but I wasn't sure I could pick it up! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Yes you can have a weapon and ammo together if you have a personal protection permit (only is some states) or a Federal handgun permit.  I got a Federal handgun permit when I was in the Army and pay $57/year to renew it.  I don't even own a gun anymore.  *****Whatever you do, do NOT carry a gun into Cook County, Ill.  (Chicago)  You will go to jail for a very long time.  Off duty cops aren't even allowed to carry.  I don't know this from experience, thank God!******<BR>I carried a .45 with me when I drove a truck for protection and was never bothered with my permit.  Remember that a personal protection (concealed weapon) permit from one state is not necessarity valid in another.  I know this is slightly off-topic, but I'd hate for someone to end up in jail.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Racer 1||12-22-1999||01:30 AM||||Here in NM you can carry a loaded gun anywhere except a liquor selling place,mall,bank. I can carry a load gun anywhere in my car. Because our law says your vehicle is a extension of your property. I just transport my guns to the shooting range in my car. Although one time about 5 years ago I walked out to the desert "about 2 miles from my house" with my SKS rifle and when I got out to the desert a police car pulled up and told me to put down the weapon. So I did put down the gun  it wasnt loaded he inspected it said nice gun and I went walking on even got some honks with thumbs up with cars driving by. ||||reg||10
Z||000027||ChadMan||12-22-1999||08:40 AM||||I love the Southwest.  Great weather, friendly people, and lots of car, motorcycle and gun nuts.  If it weren't for family and friends here in the Buckeye State I'd move out there in a heartbeat.<P>Some S.O.B. was egging cars in my apt parking lot this past fall.  He got my 88 Coupe 4 times and my 86 SE V6 once (which prompted me to store it, then sell it, because I worried at work all day about the Fiero I'd left home).  <P>So I have a special place in my heart (and my clip) for Fiero vandals and thieves.||||reg||1
Z||000028||DJRice||12-22-1999||08:43 AM||||Richard - That particular incident occured in my Olds, but your right about the change interfering with moving parts in the shifter area.  <P>Fiero56 - I had no idea that those of us with paintball guns had to follow the same rules as with regular guns.  <P>BTW that reminds me of a good paintball story.  One Saturday morning during a game, one of the guys was having car trouble and called a taxi to get him where ever he was going.  The driveway to where we were playing  was rather long and led into a heavily wooded area.  So the taxi comes rolling up right after we had completed a round of speedball.  The driver sees 20 people in Camo and headgear all with big guns and freaked out.  He tried to back out and hit a tree.  That was funny! (not to him, but everyone else enjoyed it)||||reg||1
Z||000029||fiero56||12-22-1999||10:03 AM||||Monkeyman: He has also had a 5 and 6-cell lite, but he found the 4-cell the easiest to fit through the window for beating someone on the head. Says its a good size/weight ratio.<BR>Djrice: I see your from Florida. The paintball law might just be Ohio, and other select states. I've never seen it written, but all the shops say when transporting your paintballs and gun, keep 'em seperate. <BR>The place I go paintballing is at the end of a very bumpy rutted driveway maybe 1/3 to 1/2 mile long. I end up parking my Fiero on the side of the street, and hiking to the shack. Since the place is so far out of town, I don't worry much about the Fiero.||||reg||1
